[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Git SCM Integration, Webhooks, and Polling Strategies in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Automating your CI/CD pipeline requires a reliable connection between your version control system (Git) and your automation server (Jenkins). This tutorial covers configuring Git SCM, setting up automated triggers via Webhooks, and implementing robust polling strategies."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Configuring Git SCM in Jenkins"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To allow Jenkins to pull source code from a Git repository, you must configure the Git plugin within a Freestyle project or a Pipeline job."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Navigate to your Jenkins job and click on 'Configure'."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scroll down to the 'Source Code Management' section and select 'Git'."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enter your repository URL in the 'Repository URL' field (e.g., https://github.com/your-org/your-repo.git)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Add or select credentials with appropriate read access to your Git repository."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Specify the branch to build under 'Branches to build' (e.g., */main or */master)."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Implementing Declarative Pipeline Git SCM"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"For Pipeline jobs, you can define the Git checkout directly inside your Jenkinsfile using the checkout step or the agent directives."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                git branch: 'main',
                    credentialsId: 'github-secret-id',
                    url: 'https://github.com/your-org/your-repo.git'
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Polling Strategies (Poll SCM)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Polling is a pull-based strategy where Jenkins periodically checks the Git repository for changes. If changes are detected, a new build is triggered. This is configured using cron-like syntax."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"In your job configuration, check 'Poll SCM'."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enter a schedule string in the 'Schedule' text box. For example, 'H/5 * * * *' polls every 5 minutes."},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"While polling is easy to set up, it introduces load on your Git server and latency in your pipeline execution."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Webhooks: Event-Driven Triggers"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Webhooks provide an event-driven mechanism. Instead of Jenkins asking Git if changes occurred, your Git provider (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket) notifies Jenkins instantly via an HTTP POST request whenever a push or pull request occurs."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"In Jenkins, check the option 'GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling' (or the equivalent for your Git provider)."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure your Jenkins instance is reachable from the internet, or is accessible by your internal Git server."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"In your GitHub repository settings, navigate to 'Webhooks' and add a new webhook."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Set the Payload URL to your Jenkins server endpoint: http://<your-jenkins-ip>:8080/github-webhook/"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Set the content type to 'application/json' and select 'Just the push event' or individual events as needed."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Combining Git SCM integration with Webhooks provides a fast, scalable, and resource-efficient CI/CD workflow. Reserve Polling (Poll SCM) as a fallback mechanism for environments where network restrictions prevent inbound webhook requests to Jenkins."}]
